  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1146 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nToday through Tuesday: Dry and Above Normal Temperatures\n\nMild fall weather continues for today and Tuesday across the \nUpper Midwest. Surface high pressure is progged to remain over \nthe region on both days which will keep conditions dry for both \nof these days. Temperatures will continue to remain above normal\nas H850 forecast temperatures are in the 8-10 Celsius range. \nThe latest 08/01z NBM indicates medium to high (60-95%) chances \nfor highs to reach or exceed 60 degrees today for areas along \nand south of I-94. That potential decreases a bit on Tuesday and\nthe spacial coverage of those probabilities greatly decreases. \nMid level moisture increases this afternoon and once again on \nTuesday afternoon, leading to cloud cover on both days. The \ncloud cover lingers into Wednesday morning out ahead of an upper\nlevel trough digging south.\n\nWednesday and Thursday: Light Rain Chances and a Cool Down\n\nAn H300 trough will dig south out of Canada mid-week, leading \nto chances for light preciptiation and much cooler temperatures.\nThe surface low pressure system associated with the trough is \nexpected to pass just to the south of the region. The lift \nprovided by this weather system should generate light \npreciptiation on the northern side of the surface low on \nWednesday. The potential that he precipitation remains light is \nvery high and the newest NBM indicates that the 90th percentile \nprecipitation amounts up to 0.25-0.35 inches. These amounts \nhave come up slightly, likely in response to higher confidence \nin at least some precipitation across the area. Light rain is \nthe favored precipitation type with this system as model \nguidance indicates the push of cold air is not well timed with \nthe majority of the precipitation. If some light precipitation \ncan linger into Wednesday night or Thursday, this would the be \ntimeframe for the potential for some snowflakes to mix in. No \naccumulation of any wintry precipitation is expected even if \nlight snow were to occur.\n\nThe cold air mass will be firmly in place over the Upper \nMidwest throughout the day on Thursday. H850 temperatures are \nexpected to drop down to around -5 to -8 Celsius during the day.\nCurrent NBM highs for Thursday are in the low to mid 40s, but I\nwould not be surprised if those are a bit too high given NBM \nbias correction leaning temperatures on the warm side given how \nwarm we have been recently. \n\nNext Weekend: High Pressure Sets In \n\nA Canadian high pressure is expected to sink south into the \nUpper Midwest by Friday, dominating much of the weather pattern \nacross the Central CONUS by Saturday. This will keep conditions \ndry so apart from the light rain chances on Wednesday, a dry \nforecast is on tap for the area through at least next Monday. \nTemperatures will be at their coolest Thursday into Friday with \nsome recovery expected by Saturday into the upper 40s.",
